    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Collagen may help brittle nails, but the evidence is one small study: 25 women taking 2.5g of collagen peptides daily for 24 weeks.</strong> It reported 12% faster nail growth and 42% fewer broken nails, but it had no placebo group, so it cannot separate collagen from the effect of simply taking part.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The trial:</strong> open-label, single centre, 25 women, a branded collagen peptide at 2.5g a day. Its own authors called for larger placebo-controlled studies.</li>
        <li><strong>Minimum fair test:</strong> fingernails grow about 3.5mm a month and take three to six months to replace fully, so judge any change at 24 weeks, not four.</li>
        <li><strong>Biotin:</strong> the brittle-nail studies used 2.5mg a day and were small and uncontrolled too. Collabloom's 45mcg of biotin is a nutritional amount, about 55 times lower.</li>
        <li><strong>Check first:</strong> repeated wetting and drying, low iron, an underactive thyroid, psoriasis and fungal infection all cause brittle nails, and none of them respond to collagen.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

