          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Hexsel+bioactive+collagen+peptides+nail+growth+brittle+nails+2017" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">J Cosmet Dermatol, 2017</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">0</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Placebo groups in the trial. Every participant knew she was taking collagen</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Hexsel+bioactive+collagen+peptides+nail+growth+brittle+nails+2017" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Study design</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Now the limits, which almost nobody selling collagen mentions. The trial was open-label, so
        participants and assessors knew everyone was on the supplement. It was run at a single centre.
        It had no placebo arm, which matters a lot for nails, because people who join a nail study
        also tend to start wearing gloves, moisturising and filing more carefully. And it tested one
        manufacturer's branded peptide. The authors themselves called for larger placebo-controlled
        trials, and as of this writing nobody has published one.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the honest summary is: a plausible signal, in a small uncontrolled study, at a modest dose.
        Not proof, and not a reason to expect your nails to change in a month.
      </p>

