      <h2 id="biotin">Collagen or biotin for nails?</h2>

      <p>
        Neither has strong evidence, and biotin's is older and just as thin. A 2017 review of biotin for
        nail disease found small trials reporting firmer, thicker nails, but no well-controlled studies,
        and called for proper trials to establish whether it works and at what dose.
      </p>

      <p>
        The biotin studies that reported benefit used 2.5mg a day, often in very small groups without
        a control. One commonly cited trial reported improvement in 41 of 45 patients; another found
        a 25% increase in nail thickness in a small sample. Nail specialists point out that true biotin
        deficiency is rare outside inherited enzyme disorders, which is the main reason extra biotin
        helps fewer people than its reputation suggests.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our own label deserves the same scrutiny. Collabloom contains 45mcg of biotin, roughly 55 times
        less than the 2.5mg used in brittle-nail studies. It is a nutritional amount that contributes
        to daily intake, not a nail treatment, and we would not ask you to buy it for that. Its
        collagen is a different story: 8,000mg a day is more than three times the 2.5g used in the nail
        trial. That does not make the evidence stronger, but it does mean the dose is not the weak
        point. If you take high-dose biotin for nails, tell your doctor before any blood test, because
        large doses can distort some lab results, including thyroid and heart tests. Our article on
        <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a> covers this in more detail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="causes">Why are my nails brittle?</h2>

      <p>
        Most often because of water and chemicals, not nutrition. Brittle nails that split in layers
        at the tip are usually caused by repeated wetting and drying, which dehydrates the nail plate,
        along with detergents, nail polish remover and frequent manicures. Age makes it more common.
      </p>

      <p>
        A smaller group of brittle nails have a medical cause that needs treating, and these are worth
        ruling out before spending money on supplements:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Iron deficiency.</strong> Thin nails that curve upward like a spoon, called koilonychia, are linked with iron deficiency anaemia, which is common in Indian women.</li>
        <li><strong>Underactive thyroid.</strong> Brittle nails with fatigue, hair thinning and feeling cold are a reason for a thyroid test.</li>
        <li><strong>Psoriasis.</strong> Pitting, nail lifting, or nail changes with skin plaques or joint pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Fungal infection.</strong> Thick, yellow-brown, crumbly nails, usually toenails, need antifungal treatment, not supplements.</li>
        <li><strong>Eczema around the nails,</strong> and poor circulation in the fingers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="what-to-do">What to do for brittle nails</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Protect them from water.</strong> Cotton-lined rubber gloves for dishes and cleaning are the single most useful change.</li>
        <li><strong>Moisturise the nails and cuticles</strong> after every hand wash, with a thick cream or an oil.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep them short,</strong> file in one direction, and give them breaks from polish and acetone remover.</li>
        <li><strong>Get a blood test if there are other signs:</strong> ferritin for iron, TSH for thyroid.</li>
        <li><strong>If you try collagen,</strong> take a full daily dose of at least 2.5g for 24 weeks, keep the glove and moisturiser habits going, and photograph your nails at the start so you can judge honestly.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        See a dermatologist if a nail changes colour, lifts off, develops a dark streak, or if one nail
        changes while the others do not. Those are not brittle-nail problems.
      </p>

