      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help brittle nails?</summary>
          <p>Possibly. One study of 25 women taking 2.5g of collagen peptides daily for 24 weeks reported 12% faster nail growth and 42% fewer broken nails, but it had no placebo group and was run at a single centre. It is a plausible signal, not proof.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does collagen take to work on nails?</summary>
          <p>Expect at least three months before seeing any change and six months to judge it. Fingernails grow about 3.5mm a month and take three to six months to replace fully, so new nail has to grow out before any effect shows.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take for nails?</summary>
          <p>The only nail trial used 2.5g of collagen peptides a day for 24 weeks. There is no evidence that a higher dose works better for nails, and no evidence for doses below that.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is biotin or collagen better for nails?</summary>
          <p>Neither has strong evidence. Biotin studies used 2.5mg a day in small, mostly uncontrolled groups, and true biotin deficiency is rare. Collabloom's 45mcg of biotin is a nutritional amount, about 55 times lower than those studies used.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What causes brittle nails?</summary>
          <p>Most often repeated wetting and drying, detergents, acetone and frequent manicures. Iron deficiency, an underactive thyroid, psoriasis and fungal infection are medical causes worth ruling out.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What deficiency causes brittle nails?</summary>
          <p>Iron deficiency is the most important one to check, especially if nails are thin and spoon-shaped. Thyroid underactivity can also cause brittle nails. A blood test for ferritin and TSH is more useful than guessing with supplements.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen make nails grow faster?</summary>
          <p>In the one trial that measured it, nail growth rate rose by 12% over 24 weeks on 2.5g a day. That is a small change, and without a placebo group it is not certain how much of it was due to collagen.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

